From 918123f238c4944e5126f5ecc749de484f2ce0c8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Harsha Nanda <harshanandak@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 16:20:48 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] fix(shepherd): treat proven PID reuse as dead across watcher,
 controller, and legacy recovery

Liveness was still "a process with this number exists" everywhere except the
legacy import branch. When a watcher or controller died and its PID was inherited
by an unrelated process, recovery deferred to the stranger forever and the PR
stayed unwatched: recoverDeadWatcher/recoverDeadStarting re-probed the bare PID
and rejected with pid_live, and a legacy entry with a proven-reused PID fell
through to a permanent blocked/legacy_lossy row that is never rechecked.

Introduce lib/pr-monitor/process-identity.js with processIdentityAlive(), which
classifies a PID against a marker its owner itself wrote: alive, dead, reused, or
unknown. Apply it at the controller probe (marker: the row's updatedAt), the
watcher probe (marker: the watcher-written heartbeatAt), and legacy import
(marker: the legacy startedAt). Reuse proof travels with the owner row into
recoverDeadWatcher/recoverDeadStarting as pidReuseProven; the authority
transaction re-derives the proof itself and still rejects pid_live when the flag,
the marker, or the start-time probe is missing. An open PR whose legacy PID is
proven reused now imports as a recoverable starting row adopted by the migrating
controller instead of blocked/legacy_lossy.

An unknown process start time (every non-Linux platform today) keeps the previous
bare-PID behaviour exactly.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
